    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` and wrote 49 pytest tests in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` passes all of them. Each client keeps a queue of `(time, cost)` entries, and entries older than `window_seconds` are pruned before every check. `allow` records only accepted requests, `remaining` and `retry_after` record nothing, and a single limiter-wide clock rejects any `now` that goes backwards. Invalid `limit`, `window_seconds`, `cost`, or non-numeric time raise `ValueError`, and `bool` is rejected wherever an int is expected. `retry_after` walks the client's entries oldest-first to find the first expiry at which the cost fits. The tests cover the boundary at exactly `window_seconds`, weighted costs, per-client isolation, and the clock rule. I could not measure line coverage because the shell is limited to the test command; by inspection, the one line not exercised is an unreachable fallback at the end of `retry_after`, marked `# pragma: no cover`, so I expect coverage to be above 90%. The gate ignores that pragma, so that line will count as a miss, but it is a single line out of about 60.",
